{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/481977-scenes-as-turns-or-putting-an-end-to-what-are-you-doing-now","id":481977,"title":"Scenes as turns, or, putting an end to \"What are you doing now?\"","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-04-15T23:03:32-07:00","id":481977,"title":"Scenes as turns, or, putting an end to \"What are you doing now?\"","content":"One thing I find very draining about conventional roleplaying is the almost real-time pacing it often has. When a player's turn comes up the GM asks &quot;what are you doing?&quot; with the expectation that the player's timeframe corresponds with everyone else's (even if they're away from the other PC's), and continues almost immediately from where that' player's last turn left off. This makes for slow and boring gameplay in my opinion. No other story medium works this way. Rather than advancing the plot and the timeline, this style causes people to play the doldrums of daily existence, and compresses the game timeline to an interlude that becomes absurd when it's time to spend Experience.<br /> <br /> The time intervals given by White Wolf suggest that the game is not intended to be played this way, rather as a traditional story narrative--Scenes, Chapters, etc. Yet even with White Wolf games I see (and, admittedly, have done myself out of habit) the languorous churn of &quot;what are you doing now?&quot;. I think a lot of the resistance to changing this dynamic is the greater responsibility placed on the players. If on their turn a player has to start a scene* that advances the narrative instead of just stumbling forward in time, the player is more responsible for the overall development of the story. It becomes a collaborative endeavor rather than a ride for bleary-eyed players provided by the storyteller. A great model for this structure is the Coenesque <i>Fiasco</i>. It may seem like something is lost in immersion with the abandonment of real-time--but I think a more engaging story increases immersion.<br /> <br /> So my question to you is, have many of you had success in playing the more narrative-driven way I've described? Have your groups been able to play narratively with no overt change from the typical style?<br /> <br /> *I think it's good to alternate these with ST-initiated scenes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T03:12:34-07:00","id":482181,"title":"","content":"So... how do you imagine handling combat in that paradigm?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T08:38:38-07:00","id":482422,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/481977-scenes-as-turns-or-putting-an-end-to-what-are-you-doing-now#post481977\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing I find very draining about conventional roleplaying is the almost real-time pacing it often has.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not really sure how this is a &quot;conventional&quot; RPG thing; it sounds more like a style with the groups you've played with thing.<br /> <br /> In the majority of my groups, the &quot;almost real-time pacing&quot; has always been a factor of the majority of the time we're sitting down and playing is when we're focusing on narrative-important bits. &quot;This is the big dungeon raid to get our reward from the king,&quot; &quot;This is the major court event where the Prince will try to eliminate his political rivals,&quot; &quot;We've collected all the materials and we need to perform the ritual before it's too late,&quot; and so on. All the daily minutae, is regulated to downtime which gets a bit of coverage at the beginning and end of the session, with a time frame set by the GM. &quot;It's been two weeks since the raid on the Technocratic lab, besides healing and restocking, what else have your characters been working on?&quot;<br /> <br /> GM initiated plots vs. player initiated plots to me are a separate issue. It's very dependent on your group's style. Some people want to &quot;ride the rails,&quot; more than others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T12:36:31-07:00","id":482641,"title":"","content":"This has not been a problem I have ever noticed. Outside of combat, we always use basically real time pacing. If the group is together this is simple and straight forward. If the group is divided, I run one scene with one group and then a scene with the other unless they are directly affecting each other somehow, then it's all lumped into one scene.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"<a href=http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png target=_blank>http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png</a>\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"<a href=http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png target=_blank>http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png</a>\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2323-killersquirrel59","name":"killersquirrel59","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"558"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T13:05:47-07:00","id":482664,"title":"","content":"I see your point to a degree. We call it the &quot;every bleeding minute&quot; style. It definitely is something we try to avoid for long-running plot oriented games. However, in shorter run, objective oriented, especially when there is a time crunch, the &quot;every bleeding minute&quot; style really works best. Even then though, it's often important to say something about everyone is situated doing their thing until &lt;insert event&gt; happens.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T13:46:06-07:00","id":482732,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/481977-scenes-as-turns-or-putting-an-end-to-what-are-you-doing-now?p=482664#post482664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I see your point to a degree. We call it the &quot;every bleeding minute&quot; style. It definitely is something we try to avoid for long-running plot oriented games. However, in shorter run, objective oriented, especially when there is a time crunch, the &quot;every bleeding minute&quot; style really works best. Even then though, it's often important to say something about everyone is situated doing their thing until &lt;insert event&gt; happens.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good term. I like it. Definitely something to be avoided unless every second counts though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"<a href=http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png target=_blank>http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png</a>\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"<a href=http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png target=_blank>http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png</a>\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2323-killersquirrel59","name":"killersquirrel59","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"558"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T09:21:07-07:00","id":483613,"title":"","content":"We tend to play with the &quot;every bleeding minute&quot; style, nice name btw, with occasional downtime between sessions or when the game goes to a longer hiatus but then again most of our plots tend to require a but faster acting than doing things only few days, or weeks etc., apart. So it works for us. And is quite dependent upon the game, if playing a game where we are traveling long journeys with ships, spaceships etc, we tend to skip weeks at a time naturally only focusing on times of urgency, a storm, pirate attack, government attack, sea/space monster attack etc.<br /> <br /> Nut like with anything in this hobby YMMV and what works for us may not do so for you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T10:40:26-07:00","id":483708,"title":"","content":"In different games, it has worked out very differently. Right now, while waiting for M20 to come out to start a new Mage game, we are playing a mission-oriented Vampire mini-chronicle (literally &quot;the Prince said do this&quot;) that is a spin-off of an older, very long running and successful Mage game. It's pretty much &quot;every-bleedin' minute&quot;, except that I have had reduced feeding to dice rolls with some colorful after-the-rolls explanation of what happened. Previously, since we hadn't played VtM before with this particular group or for a very long time with the veteran players, I would GUSH in lurid detail through the feeding scenes, but, we've all &quot;gotten it&quot; by now.<br /> <br /> In a SoE space opera game, we would cover significant chunks of time in transit from planet to planet in very brief exchanges about what each character was doing to deal with the boredom and some fancy descriptions of exactly what it meant that the pilot had rolled 4 successes for the transit. But when they would decide to take a transdimensional short-cut, it would definitely shift to &quot;every bleeding minute&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-18T00:10:21-07:00","id":484333,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/481977-scenes-as-turns-or-putting-an-end-to-what-are-you-doing-now?p=482181#post482181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So... how do you imagine handling combat in that paradigm?</div> </div> </div> </div>I revert to &quot;every bleeding minute&quot; or rather &quot;every bleeding three seconds.&quot; With combat the rules dictate the pacing, for good reason--at other times there is more freedom. The trouble is, it's easy to treat everything like it's as temporally rigid as combat.<br /> <br /> @Ajax, re: &quot;every bleeding minute,&quot; I like the term. I like having a term, because it makes it easier to switch between modes and let players know you're doing so. You are right that there are some situations where EBM might be appropriate. There are also fast-paced times when a bit too much gets skipped over, where I wish it was easier for players to insert quick bits of dialog that develop their characters. Even in the climax of <i>Serenity</i> Simon finds time to tell Kaylee he regrets not having boned her.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-19T01:53:01-07:00","id":485222,"title":"","content":"Oh. I don't think I ever used the turn structure outside of Combat. I never really thought you were supposed to :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}